Aphthous Stomatitis
Common Infections and Afflictions
Painful round or oval ulcers, Yellow-gray center with red halo, Non-keratinized mucosa location

VISIT CONTEXT
Case ID: CIA-010
Reason for Visit: Severe oral pain preventing eating and drinking.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage indicated an acute presentation of severe, debilitating oral ulcerations that have caused an absolute cessation of oral fluid intake for the past 18 hours due to intense odynophagia. Given the rapid trajectory of the pain and the imminent risk of clinical dehydration, an urgent home assessment is required to evaluate hydration status, definitively exclude herpetic gingivostomatitis, and aggressively institute topical analgesia. This level of intervention is necessary to restore the patient's ability to hydrate and strictly cannot be delayed until the next standard operating period, satisfying criteria for MBS urgent after-hours item numbers 585-600.

SUBJECTIVE
Chief Complaint: These ulcers in my mouth are so agonizingly painful that I can't even swallow a sip of tap water without wincing.
History of Present Illness: Symptoms initiated 3 days ago with a mild tingling sensation on the inner lower lip, which rapidly progressed into three distinct, highly painful oral ulcers. The patient reports the pain peaked today (day three), making speech difficult and preventing any solid or liquid intake. The ulcers are well-circumscribed and described as burning and stinging. The patient reports a recurrent nature of similar lesions every few months, usually during periods of high stress, but notes this outbreak is exceptionally severe. Denies any preceding blister or vesicle formation, systemic fevers, or localized swollen glands.
Functional Status: Independent, but currently debilitated by localized pain and evolving fatigue from poor intake.
Social/Safety Concerns: Living alone. Moderate risk of dehydration due to severe pain-induced fluid restriction.

OBJECTIVE
Vitals: BP: 115/75 mmHg | HR: 90 bpm | Temp: 36.9 C | RR: 16 bpm | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 68 Kg
General: Alert, well-perfused, but reluctant to speak or open the mouth fully due to pain. Oral mucosa appears slightly dry.
Recent/Relevant Labs: None available. Normal serum vitamin B12, folate, and iron profiles would be expected in routine outpatient workups to rule out underlying deficiencies assisting in confirming the diagnosis, but are not required for acute management.
Physical Exam: Intraoral examination reveals three distinct, highly painful, well-circumscribed oval ulcers located on the non-keratinized mucosa of the buccal cavity and lower labial mucosa. The lesions feature a prominent yellow-gray pseudomembranous center surrounded by a distinct, bright erythematous halo. Maximum ulcer diameter is approximately 5mm. Hard palate, gingiva, and dorsal tongue (keratinized mucosa) are completely clear. No vesicular or bullous lesions observed. Submandibular and cervical lymph nodes are non-palpable and non-tender.
Environmental Observations: Readily available access to fluids, but none have been consumed recently.
Medication Reconciliation: No regular medications. Patient has tried over-the-counter paracetamol with zero relief of the topical mucosal pain.

ASSESSMENT
Primary Diagnosis: Aphthous Stomatitis - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:
Herpes Simplex Virus (HSV) Gingivostomatitis - Ruled out definitively as lesions are exclusively located on non-keratinized mucosa, lack a vesicular prodrome, and there is no involvement of the keratinized gingiva or hard palate.
Oral Candidiasis (Thrush) - Unlikely due to the discrete, sharply demarcated nature of the ulcerations with inflammatory halos, rather than diffuse, wipeable white curdy plaques.
Hand, Foot, and Mouth Disease (Coxsackievirus) - Ruled out due to the complete absence of any concurrent maculopapular or vesicular rash on the palms, soles, or buttocks, and the absence of systemic viral symptoms.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate (Living alone with poor fluid intake)

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY
The patient's acute cessation of fluid intake secondary to excruciating mucosal pain posed a direct and imminent threat of dehydration. Urgent on-site assessment was mandated to perform a detailed intraoral examination, confirming the presence of severe but benign aphthous ulcerations rather than a widespread infectious process like HSV. By directly applying and prescribing potent topical corticosteroid pastes and local anesthetics, the immediate pain reflex was blocked, allowing the patient to successfully resume oral hydration during the consultation. This immediate stabilization prevented an inevitable deterioration requiring hospital-based intravenous fluid resuscitation, fulfilling the criteria for an urgent after-hours MBS assessment.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN
Clinical Interventions: Administered topical 2% viscous lidocaine strictly to the ulcer beds to facilitate immediate fluid intake. Prescribed Triamcinolone acetonide 0.1% dental paste, to be applied gently to the ulcers 2 to 3 times daily after meals and at bedtime. Advised Paracetamol 1000mg strictly every 6 hours.
Positive Behavioral Change: To maintain hydration without triggering severe pain, use a wide-bore plastic straw placed past the front teeth to bypass the ulcers on the lips and front cheeks when drinking. This mechanical bypass physically separates the fluid from the open nerve endings, allowing you to consume a full glass of water without wincing.
Home-Based Services: None required.
DME/Safety: None required.
Follow-up: Review with regular General Practitioner in 7-10 days if ulcers have not significantly healed, or to arrange routine hematinic blood tests (B12, iron, folate).

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN
Present to the Emergency Department if you are completely unable to pass urine for 12 hours, become profoundly dizzy upon standing, or develop a high fever and swollen neck glands.

PATIENT INFORMATION
Disclaimer: Aphthous ulcers are a clinical diagnosis; non-healing ulcers persisting beyond 3 weeks require mandatory biopsy to exclude malignancy.
Clinical Course: The acute, severe pain typically peaks on days 3-4 and will rapidly diminish once the topical steroid paste is initiated. The lesions themselves heal spontaneously without scarring in 7 to 14 days.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE
Seek immediate review if the ulcers rapidly multiply, if they spread to the outside of your lips, or if you begin to feel generally unwell with fevers and chills.

PATIENT EDUCATION
Treatment Explanation: These ulcers are a localized inflammatory overreaction, not an infection. The prescribed dental paste contains a steroid that acts as a direct fire extinguisher on the inflammation, reducing both the pain and the time it takes to heal.
Actionable Steps: Do not rub the steroid paste into the sore; simply dab a small amount over the ulcer and allow it to melt into a protective film. Strictly avoid highly acidic (citrus, tomatoes), heavily spiced, or physically rough/salty foods (like potato chips), as these will chemically and mechanically strip away the healing layer and cause intense pain.

